753 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The Value vs. Complexity Model is an essential tool in a product manager's toolkit for effective prioritisation. The challenge lies in balancing limited resources while aiming to deliver high value, especially when customer data or market research is scant. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
754 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (In this model, two dimensions are considered: 'Business Value' and 'Implementation Complexity'. Initiatives are evaluated and plotted on a quadrant that is segmented into four areas: high value-low complexity, high value-high complexity, low value-low complexity, and low value-high complexity. The objective is simple but powerful: Identify initiatives that offer the maximum value for the minimum effort. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
755 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3236 0 R
/ActualText (High value, low complexity: The "low-hanging fruits." These initiatives should be your top priority as they offer immediate business value with the least effort involved. These are your quick wins. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
756 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3237 0 R
/ActualText (High value, high complexity: Worth prioritizing but require substantial resources. The decision to go ahead often depends on ROI considerations and long-term strategic alignment. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
757 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3238 0 R
/ActualText (Low value, low complexity: These are the 'nice-to-haves.' They can be executed during downtime or when resources become momentarily available, but they aren't urgent. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
758 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3239 0 R
/ActualText (Low value, high complexity: The danger zone. These initiatives should usually be avoided as they consume substantial resources while offering minimal returns. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
759 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Each initiative's value score should consider )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
