/S /P
/ActualText (New Product Development:  Focus high-impact features that can give you a competitive edge right out of the gate. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
771 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Resource Constraints: Identify what can be realistically accomplished, ensuring the team focuses only on what brings the most value. )
/Pg 16 0 R
>>
endobj
772 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Objective Assessment: Sanity check, cutting through team biases or unwarranted enthusiasm around particular initiatives. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
773 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Summary)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
774 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The Value vs. Complexity Model is a strategic exercise that brings alignment among stakeholders and clarity to the roadmap. For product managers striving to make data-driven, high-impact decisions, mastering this model can be a game-changer. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
775 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Traditional models like cost-benefit analysis offer some guidance, but they often fall short in factoring in customer delight and satisfaction. This is where the KANO Model shines. Named after Dr. Noriaki Kano, this model provides a framework that places customer satisfaction at the forefront, enabling product teams to make strategic decisions on what features to develop, enhance, or eliminate. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
776 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Understanding the KANO Model )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
777 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Unlike conventional prioritization frameworks, the KANO Model categorizes features based on customer reactions, divided into five main types: Basic \(Threshold\), Performance, Excitement, Indifferent, and Dissatisfaction.  This approach emphasizes the dual criteria of customer satisfaction and implementation investment, making it a compelling model to use in various product life cycles. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
778 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Basic \(Threshold\) Features: )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
779 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
780 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Performance Features: )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
781 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (These are the features that customers are aware they want and will likely compare against competitors. The investment in performance features correlates directly with an increase in customer satisfaction. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
782 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Excitement Features: )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
783 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (These are the "Wow" factors, features that customers didn't know they wanted but end up delighting them immensely. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
784 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Features that induce indifference or dissatisfaction are best avoided, as they either don't affect customer choices or lead to negative experiences. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
785 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (How It Works)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
786 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The process starts with an internal discussion within the product team to identify and categorize potential new features. After this, direct customer feedback is sought through surveys, interviews, and other channels to validate or adjust these categories.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
