796 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The KANO Model offers a comprehensive yet straightforward way for product managers to prioritize features based on the degree of customer satisfaction they bring. Its adaptability and focus on customer input make it a robust framework for any product manager aiming to build a product that not only satisfies but delights the end-user.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
797 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (11)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
798 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
799 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The Four Pillars of RICE)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
800 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (1. Reach: This is a numerical estimate of the number of people your initiative is expected to affect over a specific timeframe. Whether you measure reach in terms of customer transactions, free-trial signups, or user engagement, your reach score becomes the benchmark for the project's potential scope.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
801 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (2. Impact: This quantifies the extent to which the initiative will affect the users it reaches. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
802 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Impact can be hard to measure, but it's crucial. Intercom recommends a tiered system, ranging from 'massive impact' \(3\) to 'minimal impact' \(0.25\), to assist in gauging this nebulous element.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
803 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (3. Confidence: This represents how certain you are about your estimates for Reach and Impact. If your reach score is based on solid data but the impact is more of a gut feeling, the confidence score balances this out. Scores can range from high \(100%\) to low \(50%\). Anything below 50% is considered a 'moonshot,' requiring reevaluation.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
804 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
805 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Why Use RICE Model?)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
806 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (RICE compiles various factors into a single numerical value, enabling product managers to make decisions based on data rather than intuition. Thus data-driven decisions with minimized bias can be achieved. The score provides a quantifiable rationale for why certain projects are prioritized, making it easier to defend these choices to stakeholders like executive staff.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
807 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (How to Calculate the RICE Score)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
808 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The RICE score is calculated as follows:)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
809 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText ( )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
810 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Practical Tips:)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
811 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Be Consistent: Make sure you're consistent in your timeframes and units across all four categories to ensure an apples-to-apples  comparison.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
