819 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Revisit Scores: The markets and resources change, so should your RICE scores. Make it a habit to revisit and update them regularly.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
820 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Summary)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
821 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The RICE Scoring Model is an indispensable tool for any product manager looking to make informed, unbiased, and defensible decisions. It takes the guesswork out of product management, transforming it into a more scientific and successful endeavour.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
822 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
823 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Must-have \(M\): These are non-negotiable requirements that are essential for project success. If any of these elements are missing, the project is considered incomplete.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
824 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Should-have \(S\): Important but not critical for immediate project success. These elements enhance the project but are not vital.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
825 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Could-have \(C\): These are nice-to-have elements that can be sacrificed without affecting the project's core functionality.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
826 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Will-not-have \(W\): These elements are recognized but are not a priority for the current project phase.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
827 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Why Use MoSCoW?)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
828 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (MoSCoW establishes clarity among project stakeholders, making it easier to move forward. By prioritizing must-haves, the method ensures focus on Minimum Viable )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
829 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Product \(MVP\) as well as proper resource allocation.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
830 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (However, there can be Uncertainty \(items in the 'Will-not-have' category, particularly regarding whether they will ever be implemented\) and Subjectivity.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
831 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Summary)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
832 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The MoSCoW method is a simple yet effective tool for prioritising project requirements. Its straightforward categorization helps teams focus on essential elements, making it a vital asset in Agile project management. While the method is not without its limitations, its benefits make it a robust choice for project teams.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [14 15]
>>
endobj
833 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
