855 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Also known as a weighted scorecard, this model is a project management technique used to prioritize actions, features, or investments. One popular variation of this model is the Six Sigma prioritization matrix.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
856 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Understanding the Model:)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
857 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Here's a simplified guide to employing the Weighted Scoring Model in your next project:)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
858 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Step 1: List Your Options)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
859 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Begin by listing down all the potential elements or actions you're considering for the project. This could include product features, software choices, hiring options, or any other variables. The list should be exhaustive but not ranked in any particular order yet.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
860 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Step 2: Determine the Criteria)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
861 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Identify the criteria that will serve as the basis for weighing each option. This could be cost, risk, time, quality, return on investment \(ROI\), or any other metrics pertinent to your specific project.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
862 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Step 3: Assign Weights)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
863 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Assign a weight to each in percentage terms that sums up to 100%. For example, if ROI is crucial for your project, you might weigh it at 30%, while factors like cost and effort could be assigned 20% each.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
864 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Step 4: Create the Matrix)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
865 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Construct a chart where you multiply the weight of each criterion by the score you've assigned to each option for that criterion. Sum these up to get a weighted score for each option. The option with the highest weighted score should, theoretically, be the most favourable choice.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
866 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Why Use a Weighted Scoring Model?)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
867 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Objective decision-making, collaborative evaluation among stakeholders and team members, and resource optimization \(allocation\) can be achieved.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
868 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Summary)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
869 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (As projects grow increasingly complex, the need for such organized decision-making frameworks only becomes more crucial. So, when you're in a decision-making quandary, give the Weighted Scoring Model a try.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
