877 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (What is Opportunity Scoring?)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
878 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Opportunity Scoring is a prioritisation method used to identify high-impact, yet underperforming features in a product. The approach involves asking customers to rate the importance and satisfaction levels of various product features. Those features that score high in importance but low in satisfaction are marked as opportunities.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
879 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (How to Calculate the Opportunity Score)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
880 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The Opportunity score is calculated as follows:)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
881 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Features with the highest 'importance' scores and the lowest 'satisfaction' scores are prioritised for development.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
882 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Why Use Opportunity Scoring Model?)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
883 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Broad Applications)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
884 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Although it originated in product management, Opportunity Scoring is also useful in other business areas like marketing, sales, and IT. Generally called gap analysis in these sectors, the approach helps teams weigh the relative ROI of closing identified gaps with available resources.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
885 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The Advantage over Traditional Models)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
886 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The critical differentiator in Opportunity Scoring is the emphasis on what customers are trying to achieve, rather than how they think the product should do it. Unlike traditional gap analysis that equally considers importance and satisfaction, Opportunity Scoring gives double weight to importance, thereby zeroing in on outcomes that customers value the most.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
887 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Resource Optimization)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
888 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Besides identifying the most promising areas for innovation, Opportunity Scoring can also highlight areas where resources are being wasted.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
889 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Summary)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
890 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The Opportunity Scoring Model provides a robust mechanism to identify and focus on key areas for product improvement. By honing in on what customers find important but currently dissatisfying, this model offers a targeted approach to optimising resources and maximising ROI. With its roots in outcome-driven innovation and broad applicability across various business sectors, Opportunity Scoring is more than a product management tool; it's a comprehensive strategy for sustainable business growth.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
891 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (14)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
