endobj
926 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (In today's digital landscape, the quality of the user experience \(UX\) can be the linchpin of a product's success or failure. Google, a leader in the tech industry, identified this gap and came up with an innovative, holistic framework known as the HEART Framework. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
927 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (What is the Google HEART Framework? )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
928 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Developed by Google's UX Research experts, the HEART Framework is a set of user-centered metrics aimed at providing a comprehensive assessment of the quality of UX. The framework breaks down the evaluation into five key factors: )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
929 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Happiness: Measures users' satisfaction levels through app ratings, user satisfaction surveys, and the Net Promoter Score \(NPS\).)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
930 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Engagement: Evaluates the frequency of user interactions, including session lengths and specific actions like the number of photos uploaded. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
931 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Adoption: Focuses on the number of new users and the percentage of customers using a new feature.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
932 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Retention: Assesses the percentage of users who keep returning to the product. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
933 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Task Success: Measures how efficiently and effectively users can achieve their goals within the product. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
934 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
